WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.590
At this stage in the course,

2
00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.240
you're probably familiar
with the basics of

3
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.520
databases and how they
store and manage data.

4
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.800
But it's also important
that you know how to

5
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.650
interact with databases in
order to work with data.

6
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.330
As a data engineer,

7
00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.220
you can interact
with databases using

8
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.655
Structured Query Language or
as it's more commonly known,

9
00:00:17.655 --> 00:00:21.120
SQL, also pronounced as sequel.

10
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.950
Over the next few
minutes you'll learn

11
00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:24.390
how to explain what

12
00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:28.425
SQL is and outline the
role of SQL in databases.

13
00:00:28.425 --> 00:00:30.360
What sort of interactions do

14
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.615
database engineers need to
establish with databases?

15
00:00:33.615 --> 00:00:35.670
Some of the operations
you could carry it in

16
00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.160
the data might require
you to create,

17
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.125
read, update, and delete data.

18
00:00:41.125 --> 00:00:44.690
These operations are also
known as CRUD operations.

19
00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:46.220
You might already be familiar

20
00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:47.735
with some of these operations.

21
00:00:47.735 --> 00:00:49.430
If not, don't worry,

22
00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:50.870
they'll be covered in depth at

23
00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:52.840
later stages in this course.

24
00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.260
Let's find out more about SQL.

25
00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.290
SQL is the standard
language that can

26
00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.225
be used with all databases.

27
00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.010
It's particularly useful when

28
00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:02.840
working with
relational databases,

29
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.250
which require a
language that can

30
00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.110
interact with structured data.

31
00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.630
Some examples of
relational databases that

32
00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:11.620
SQL can interact
with include MySQL,

33
00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:16.095
PostgreSQL, Oracle, and
Microsoft SQL Server.

34
00:01:16.095 --> 00:01:17.940
The next question this raises,

35
00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:20.360
is how does a
database interpret or

36
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.890
read and execute instructions
given using SQL.

37
00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:27.845
A database interprets and make
sense of SQL instructions

38
00:01:27.845 --> 00:01:32.300
with the use of a Database
Management System or DBMS.

39
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.775
As a web developer,
you'll execute

40
00:01:34.775 --> 00:01:38.585
all SQL instructions on
a database using a DBMS.

41
00:01:38.585 --> 00:01:42.200
The DBMS takes responsibility
for transforming

42
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.105
SQL instructions into a form

43
00:01:44.105 --> 00:01:47.005
that's understood by the
underlying database.

44
00:01:47.005 --> 00:01:50.195
This was just a very quick
introduction to SQL.

45
00:01:50.195 --> 00:01:51.650
At this early stage,

46
00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.270
you should be able
to explain what SQL

47
00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:56.695
is and explain the role
of SQL in databases.

48
00:01:56.695 --> 00:01:58.385
In the upcoming videos,

49
00:01:58.385 --> 00:02:00.050
you'll learn more about SQL and

50
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.000
develop a deeper understanding
of the language.